hat his attitude significantly degrades crew coordination, I would reconsider in favor of B (if his interpersonal issues are less severe) or another candidate.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_2\", \"vote\": \"Candidate A\"}", "thoughts": "We need to produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON. We must evaluate candidates based on given attributes. Summarize evidence.\n\nWe have candidate info list. Let's list positives and negatives.\n\nCandidate A:\nPositives: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities.\nNegatives: regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nCandidate B:\nPositives: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills.\nNegatives: relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues.\n\nCandidate C:\nPositives: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in a crisis, understands complicated technology.\nNegatives: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education.\n\nCandidate D:\nPositives: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously.\nNegatives: regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nNow evaluate against criteria: reliability/responsibility, technical competence, calmness/judgment under pressure, decision-making, teamwork/communication, professional development.\n\nCandidate A: strong technical (spatial vision, complex connections), leadership, anticipates danger (good judgment). Negatives: show-off (could affect teamwork), not open to new ideas (could hinder development). No explicit calmness under pressure, but anticipation suggests proactive. Teamwork may be issue.\n\nCandidate B: conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (calm), good weather assessment (technical), computer skills (technical). Weak memory for numbers (could affect navigation), nasty remarks (teamwork issue). Overall strong reliability, but teamwork issue.\n\nCandidate C: quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, understands tech (technical). Negatives: communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (development). Teamwork issues.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentration, problem solving, responsibility serious (reliability). Negatives: know-it-all (teamwork), hot temper (under pressure). No explicit calmness, hot temper could be issue under stress.\n\nWe need provisional preference. Which candidate balances strengths and weaknesses best? Probably Candidate B: strong reliability, stress handling, technical weather assessment, conscientiousness. Weaknesses: memory for numbers and nasty remarks. Memory could be mitigated with tools, but nasty remarks affect crew cooperation heavily. Candidate C also has calmness and quick decisions, but communication issues and egocentric. Candidate A leadership but show-off and not open to ideas. Candidate D responsibility but hot temper.\n\nWhich is worst? Hot temper may be severe for long flights. Nasty remarks also severe. Communication difficulty also severe. Show-off maybe less severe. Not open to new ideas maybe moderate.\n\nThus Candidate A might be best despite show-off, because leadership, anticipation, spatial vision are critical for long-distance. Teamwork may be okay if show-off not too disruptive. No mention of stress handling, but anticipation may reduce stress.\n\nCandidate B's nasty remarks could jeopardize crew safety. I'd lean Candidate A.\n\nBut we lack info on stress handling for A. Could ask other agents about A's calmness under pressure. However round 1 we must give provisional.\n\nI'll state provisional preference Candidate A, with confidence moderate, note uncertainties: lack of info on stress handling, teamwork impact of show-off, openness to new ideas.\n\nProvide what would change: evidence of poor stress handling or severe teamwork issues, or stronger evidence for others.\n\nProceed."}
